WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.340
720, be the skateboarding champion of the world!

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:26.740
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:28.780
I'm Mike. And I'm Sean.

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:32.780
Sean, we play the games chronologically.

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:35.900
I'm sure that everyone knows that by now.

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:37.500
We, we, it's in the, it's in the intro.

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:40.160
Yeah, we, you say it a lot.

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:47.160
And even when you don't need to, when we're out and about and not recording the show, you say chronological a lot.

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:48.780
That's how I introduce myself, actually.

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:50.360
I say, I'm Michael Esposito.

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:52.520
I do a chronological exploration.

00:00:52.520 --> 00:01:11.040
However, some crazy people who have maybe explored the NES library on their own, they might be playing alphabetically through like, you know, through the means of having every ROM on their computer and just going down the line and checking out the games that way.

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:19.680
In which case, 720 is a lot, that gets a lot of exposure in terms of people who play NES emulators.

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:24.460
Yeah. I mean, it's definitely not number one, right?

00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:29.160
Like there's definitely games with numbers lower than seven.

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:36.080
Yeah. Well, no, there's eight eyes, which I think eight would be before 720, right?

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:43.440
Like in terms of how a computer alphabetizes things, the eight is, comes before 720.

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:45.440
Oh, okay.

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:46.920
Okay. I gotcha.

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:56.220
Yeah. I mean, but if you're just, if you're just going by how the file explorer works, then yeah, this is definitely at the top of my list.

00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:59.140
And I was looking forward to it for a long time because of that.

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:05.940
We're finally here in our chronological journey and we don't have too many other skateboarding games.

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:08.120
And that's kind of surprising, right?

00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:18.540
Like it's, it's the eighties skateboarding is kind of having a moment, at least in like television and film, not necessarily like on ESPN yet with the X games and all that.

00:02:18.620 --> 00:02:24.760
And there's a, I'm sure Tony Hawk is, is a thing and he's skateboarding and all, but it's not like he hasn't blown up completely yet.

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:29.860
And so I just thought there would be more skateboarding representation on the NES by now.

00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:31.640
What about you? I don't know.

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:44.620
I think like in terms of how, I mean, I know it's not, it's not easy to compare then and now because skateboarding games are now overrepresented.

00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:56.300
I think not so much in the last few years, but in the last decade, skateboarding games are kind of overrepresented in terms of like skateboarding's impact in our culture.

00:02:56.300 --> 00:03:05.300
But I think like in terms of this early NES doc that we've had, it's, it's kind of been overrepresented as well.

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:15.080
There's been like three or four games that have skateboarding at least as one of several California based sports, right?

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:17.380
I love that. California based sports.

00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:18.520
It's, you're not wrong.

00:03:18.520 --> 00:03:20.220
I mean, what was that?

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:22.700
That town and country surf designs.

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:28.060
We had that. Yeah, that had skateboarding as like, again, but it's always an obstacle course, right?

00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:31.640
It's never like, it's never like skateboarding the sport itself.

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:33.640
It's always like as a gimmick thing.

00:03:33.820 --> 00:03:37.420
Back to the Future even had Marty on the skateboard in the game.

00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:38.340
But again, obstacle course.

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:42.760
That's what I'm thinking of is like the most recent skateboarding adjacent property.

00:03:42.960 --> 00:04:01.920
Okay. However, 720 is a skateboarding game where you are kind of in a skate park hub world where you must navigate to various mini games that take place inside this hub score appropriately in them to get either a bronze, silver or gold medal.

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:06.660
And if you're lucky enough to gold medal in all four events, I think it's four.

00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:10.760
If you gold medal in all those events, you win the game.

00:04:10.940 --> 00:04:17.620
However, as easy as that might sound, keep in mind that nobody in the skate park hub wants you to even get to those mini games.

00:04:17.620 --> 00:04:32.220
They'll be standing in your way, whether they're pedestrians, cars, flying bees that I don't know why I mentioned flying, but bees that will fly towards you and shout at you that you must skate or die.

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:37.600
All these things happen to kind of deter you from being able to pull off any sort of skateboarding.

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:41.940
Were you surprised, Sean, to be in a skate park hub?

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:44.420
Well, okay.

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:46.040
So it definitely is.

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:50.060
I get the layout and the way that things look.

00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:52.500
It looks like it's supposed to be a skate park.

00:04:52.700 --> 00:05:04.320
Like it's got the, I forgot the term that's used, but the like little ramps that are, I think they're like jumpers.

00:05:04.460 --> 00:05:11.880
I don't know. The little, everything is designed to look like it's in a skate park, but it like the way that it's interacted with by the,

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:16.260
other moving objects in the game.

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:17.980
It's supposed to just look like a street.

00:05:18.280 --> 00:05:20.280
Like there's, there's people driving cars.

00:05:20.540 --> 00:05:24.420
Like you said, there's people running crazily drug fueled.

00:05:24.700 --> 00:05:26.000
They just want to knock you over.

00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:28.120
And then of course the bees.

00:05:29.080 --> 00:05:40.220
I was surprised that a lot of the things that, that you, that you see in this game in terms of the hub world and, and, and the events as well are actual like skateboarding things.

00:05:40.220 --> 00:05:50.340
Like in previous games that we've played, um, the things you'd be doing with your skateboard, weren't really the things that you'd be doing in a skateboard in real life.

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:53.940
Uh, whereas like the, the events make sense.

00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:57.840
Like I can imagine that there is like a downhill skateboard.

00:05:58.020 --> 00:06:01.000
There is the half pipe in the game.

00:06:01.240 --> 00:06:12.180
Um, it's, it, it, it seems more, more rooted in what skateboarding is instead of like what a Japanese developer that has just been tasked with making a skateboarding game thinks it is.

00:06:12.180 --> 00:06:21.080
Um, but then you have those killer bees and it's like, if you, if you're going to give me a skate park, let me skate in it instead of giving me like 30 seconds.

00:06:21.640 --> 00:06:24.420
And, uh, sorry, I'm just, I'm just rambling now.

00:06:24.420 --> 00:06:36.240
No, you bring up a, you bring up a great point because I would argue that the skate park hub world, you know, or even just city street that you're in, uh, is not exploration focused at all.

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:43.640
It actually is a little bit of a chore, uh, to, to, to navigate and to get around because they kind of just drop you off in the very middle.

00:06:44.120 --> 00:06:49.320
And granted you might go in any particular direction and you'll find one of the mini games.

00:06:49.420 --> 00:06:54.180
Like it'd be hard to almost just be like, Oh, this whole world's empty or just fill people trying to kill me.

00:06:54.280 --> 00:07:04.580
That's weird. You'll most likely stumble across something, but there's no, like, there's no like missables or fun things that happen inside the hub itself.

00:07:04.580 --> 00:07:07.440
The time limit is very tight.

00:07:07.720 --> 00:07:14.360
And like you mentioned those bees, they're going to come after you and force you to kind of either go to a mini game or take a death.

00:07:14.500 --> 00:07:22.780
And then there's this ticket system, which feels like an unnecessary restraint as well, since you can earn more tickets by going to score points, but it's hard enough.

00:07:22.840 --> 00:07:29.580
Like having the ability to get to the mini games and actually like pull off anything inside of them.

00:07:29.580 --> 00:07:38.640
So then to restrict entry into those mini games too, is like another element that it'd be like, I could understand if they were things that you picked up in the hub.

00:07:38.760 --> 00:07:43.160
Like there was just tickets lying around and you had to pick them up or something, but that's not the case.

00:07:43.260 --> 00:07:45.740
Instead it's you by scoring points.

00:07:45.740 --> 00:07:52.960
And therefore it's a little strange that you have to get the, get to the mini games to score the points, to get, to go back to the mini games.

00:07:53.520 --> 00:07:57.500
It's like a, there's duplicate gates going on here.

00:07:57.640 --> 00:08:06.920
Like I get that there should be either a time gate or a skill gate and like to move forward and to do these other events.

00:08:06.920 --> 00:08:13.060
Um, if you want the skill gate, make the player, uh, do tricks.

00:08:13.280 --> 00:08:16.900
Like, you know, you can only ollie and spin in this game.

00:08:17.100 --> 00:08:21.900
So your tricks are just those two things, but make the player do those things.

00:08:21.940 --> 00:08:27.580
And then once they do it enough, then they've proved that they can do the next event.

00:08:27.860 --> 00:08:33.140
Um, or you can do the time gate and that's what the timer is for.

00:08:33.140 --> 00:08:43.760
And that's the, and it's more, it's less so like judging if the player is like getting good enough to be given the chance to do these other events.

00:08:43.880 --> 00:08:47.100
And it's more so like, uh, did they explore enough?

00:08:47.240 --> 00:08:50.580
Did they understand the map enough to get to these events?

00:08:50.820 --> 00:09:03.120
Um, so I don't really know what they're trying to do because putting two, uh, different things that you have to fulfill, uh, on top of a otherwise like open.

00:09:03.140 --> 00:09:05.080
In environment is very annoying.

00:09:05.720 --> 00:09:08.280
So they should have just gone with one or the other.

00:09:08.520 --> 00:09:11.620
I think that either would have been fine alone.

00:09:12.580 --> 00:09:19.580
Yeah. Or even just making that skate park hub more of an obstacle course in itself to get to the mini game.

00:09:19.580 --> 00:09:21.500
So it was like its own game.

00:09:21.720 --> 00:09:28.980
And then by being able to successfully, uh, jump over obstacles or, um, dodge these enemies.

00:09:29.300 --> 00:09:37.000
That's like at the end of that, I'm thinking about like in paper boy, you know, like after you completed the game, they just like, they're like, Oh, random obstacle course.

00:09:37.120 --> 00:09:38.940
And it's just kind of a fun thing to score bonus points.

00:09:38.940 --> 00:09:43.720
If that was what this hub world was to bring you to the mini games, that might make more sense.

00:09:43.900 --> 00:09:49.820
But otherwise it's just a very, very open environment that, uh, does give you a map to reference.

00:09:50.020 --> 00:09:55.060
If you skate over the words map, but there's nothing going on inside of there.

00:09:55.160 --> 00:09:56.160
There's like a place.

00:09:56.260 --> 00:09:58.920
Yeah. There's a place to buy elbow pads, a place to buy a helmet.

00:09:58.920 --> 00:10:02.100
Uh, I'm not even sure exactly what those things do.

00:10:02.380 --> 00:10:08.720
Do you know? Well, I know that I think the skateboard makes you go faster.

00:10:08.740 --> 00:10:23.300
I don't, I don't know what the other things do that it's explained in the manual, but the quick note on these, uh, on these stores where you buy one piece of equipment, they're very weirdly designed.

00:10:23.300 --> 00:10:29.800
Uh, when you skate up to them and buy something, uh, you get like a negative response, like, Oh, we're closed.

00:10:30.360 --> 00:10:32.640
But you actually just like, just bought the thing.

00:10:33.860 --> 00:10:37.060
Um, I thought that that was very weird too.

00:10:38.060 --> 00:10:50.720
Yeah. Uh, so I, I did go to the manual here since you mentioned it and, um, the helmet shop, for instance, uh, with a new helmet, you'll be well protected when you fall and it won't take you so long to recover.

00:10:51.280 --> 00:10:52.420
Okay. That makes sense.

00:10:52.760 --> 00:10:54.740
Pretty, pretty cool. However, here's a weird thing.

00:10:54.740 --> 00:11:00.920
The shop is only closed because you bought the helmets and maybe they only sold one helmet and you're out.

00:11:00.920 --> 00:11:11.140
Right. Yeah. But no, you have to purchase when you purchase an equipment, uh, it just fills up a quarter of a pie of the picture of that item.

00:11:11.640 --> 00:11:12.760
So. Oh, wait.

00:11:12.760 --> 00:11:14.980
So I actually never upgraded anything.

00:11:15.340 --> 00:11:23.700
Yeah. It says, although you can only purchase one of each item per class, you can upgrade your equipment when you move up to the next class.

00:11:23.700 --> 00:11:39.640
So there's like a, another, like a new game plus mode element to this where like after you've moved up in class from completing all the mini games, you then go to like a harder version where you must then, you know, like upgrade your gear and do all the mini games again.

00:11:39.900 --> 00:11:43.880
But now with a tougher difficulty, that's, well, I wouldn't call that new game plus.

00:11:44.240 --> 00:11:45.740
I would just call that level two.

00:11:46.160 --> 00:11:46.960
Okay. Yeah.

00:11:47.400 --> 00:11:49.620
Much better. Why didn't they ever think of that?

00:11:49.880 --> 00:11:56.480
Right. It'd be cool if they, you know, cause they're thinking about buying gear and having tickets and stuff like that.

00:11:56.660 --> 00:12:06.480
The money in this game in general just kind of felt like a, um, a secondary element that I, I never thought about like, oh, I, where's, uh, how much money do I have?

00:12:06.580 --> 00:12:09.560
Can I afford this? I never really looked at the menus at all.

00:12:09.840 --> 00:12:15.280
Uh, there's like a little UI that tells you your current, uh, score and your, uh, how much money you have.

00:12:15.280 --> 00:12:31.960
Be cool if there was some kind of not carnival, but like something going on as a reason to like have money and tickets be involved in this world and not just like, it's a very dangerous town that he lives in and every, everything costs money.

00:12:31.960 --> 00:12:34.500
And, uh, fun is, uh, fun is tickets.

00:12:35.300 --> 00:12:43.060
Yeah. I, I mean, the, the whole question of, uh, like, are, do the tickets make sense?

00:12:43.300 --> 00:12:45.520
Like, why are we using them?

00:12:45.520 --> 00:12:50.300
Like it just really saying that there isn't a lot of content in this game.

00:12:50.520 --> 00:13:05.340
And if they just let you do everything one to one to one to one, uh, ramp, slalom, downhill and jump, uh, you'd be done with at least the first batch of this game in like 10 minutes.

00:13:05.340 --> 00:13:13.460
So I get that the tickets have to be there to at least make you like putz around in the open area for a bit.

00:13:13.920 --> 00:13:30.940
Um, but at least in the manual, I feel like there's a little bit of context given, like it's not a carnival, like you say, but I think there's like some, like, it's sort of a, like you finally made it to like one of the big skate parks in the world.

00:13:30.940 --> 00:13:32.260
And you're going to prove it.

00:13:32.380 --> 00:13:35.760
You're going to prove to the world that you're a skater, a real skater boy.

00:13:35.920 --> 00:13:39.600
I think it's something like that, but I don't know for sure.

00:13:40.480 --> 00:13:46.480
Well, Sean, the game's message, uh, if the bees come after you is to skate or die.

00:13:46.980 --> 00:13:51.360
And we have played skate or die on the NES.

00:13:51.720 --> 00:13:54.540
The two games are not, uh, at all related.

00:13:54.600 --> 00:13:56.220
Uh, they're not by the same developer.

00:13:56.220 --> 00:14:03.220
And that skate or die thing is just like a, a funny coincidence, but it makes me think about going back to skate or die for a minute.

00:14:03.280 --> 00:14:05.780
Now, I think we said a lot of nice things about that game.

00:14:05.920 --> 00:14:19.320
We recorded that episode and that has the total opposite of a hub world where, yeah, you start in the skate shop and then you come out of it, but there's nothing else to do other than just skate to the event that you want to go to.

00:14:19.320 --> 00:14:28.460
So it's a, you know, it's like a very, very small, uh, one screen image where it's just all of the events and you just kind of skate down that road and then you're there.

00:14:29.460 --> 00:14:36.620
You know, I think a hub world could have been a really cool thing and a way to differentiate yourself from skate or die, which is already on the console.

00:14:36.620 --> 00:14:44.060
But now seeing both of them in practice, I kind of just like that skater die just went forward and said like, yeah, just skate up right to the event you want to do.

00:14:44.220 --> 00:14:46.160
And then the focus is on the mini games.

00:14:46.260 --> 00:14:48.880
Now, granted they had more mini games, but still.

00:14:48.880 --> 00:14:57.020
Well, I think you would have felt differently and I would have felt differently if there wasn't this pressure to get out of the hub world.

00:14:57.220 --> 00:15:08.920
Like that, that seems like a cool place to just like, like work on your timing for jumps or work on like, oh, can I get this many spins in before I fall?

00:15:08.920 --> 00:15:17.860
At least in terms of the mini games that share the same, uh, like perspective and, uh, controls.

00:15:18.400 --> 00:15:32.100
Uh, it seems like a cool place to just like practice, but because these bees will come after you, it removes any actual usefulness, uh, or enjoyment for this hub world.

00:15:32.100 --> 00:15:41.280
So yeah, right now it's useless and I prefer just to go the skate or die method, but if there wasn't any, if it was just a place to hang, I think I'd like it.

00:15:41.420 --> 00:15:56.140
And if you're me and you have a fear of bees and being, uh, being stung, this is a nightmarish game, even by eight bit standards, because it's not like bees are just chasing you or one big bee is chasing you.

00:15:56.140 --> 00:16:00.360
It's a bunch of bees chasing you from, from seemingly nowhere.

00:16:00.680 --> 00:16:03.180
In a cloud of bees. Right. As a cloud of bees, but they come out of nowhere.

00:16:03.180 --> 00:16:06.100
Right. And then they are magnetized to you.

00:16:06.140 --> 00:16:09.960
But then as they get closer, they turn into a giant syringe.

00:16:10.840 --> 00:16:12.500
And then. Yeah, I could have done without that.

00:16:12.700 --> 00:16:17.320
Right. And then when they, when they do get you, they turn into the, uh, skull and bones.

00:16:17.660 --> 00:16:22.040
So it's like, wow, this is like really scary for, uh.

00:16:22.280 --> 00:16:23.740
Why the syringe though?

00:16:24.000 --> 00:16:25.080
Yeah. Why the syringe?

00:16:25.200 --> 00:16:26.840
Like, what was that? Was that poison?

00:16:28.260 --> 00:16:30.320
I think, I think, I don't know.

00:16:30.560 --> 00:16:31.700
Well, they're stingers, right?

00:16:31.840 --> 00:16:33.520
So they're like, you know, the big needle.

00:16:33.940 --> 00:16:37.860
I'd never made the connection that it was injecting something into me.

00:16:38.020 --> 00:16:40.280
You know, I just like, oh, you don't want to be stung by a bee.

00:16:40.460 --> 00:16:44.560
Not like you don't want to have like cyanide injected into your bloodstream.

00:16:44.780 --> 00:16:47.240
That's, that's, that's sort of what I get from that.

00:16:47.400 --> 00:16:48.380
But I don't know. Yeah.

00:16:48.740 --> 00:16:53.940
I just don't think we ever needed the bees, but the obvious reason for why we have the bees,

00:16:53.940 --> 00:16:58.360
and I usually save this for the sequels and spinoffs section, but 720 was first an arcade

00:16:58.360 --> 00:17:04.680
game. So that, you know, the bees were there to kind of implement like a put more quarters in kind

00:17:04.680 --> 00:17:07.340
of thing, uh, and play the game.

00:17:07.340 --> 00:17:11.020
You know, you couldn't just, uh, that's why the ticket system was probably in there too.

00:17:11.280 --> 00:17:12.640
All this stuff is from the arcade game.

00:17:12.800 --> 00:17:16.200
However, now I've paid the full price to have it at home.

00:17:16.460 --> 00:17:20.780
I don't think that I need the bee restriction anymore at all.

00:17:21.720 --> 00:17:23.020
Yeah. No, okay.

00:17:23.060 --> 00:17:28.000
Now that you explained that, I did know that it was an arcade game before, but I, I didn't

00:17:28.000 --> 00:17:31.840
make the connection that this was sort of a holdover, but no, that's definitely it.

00:17:31.940 --> 00:17:33.000
And it should have been removed.

00:17:33.000 --> 00:17:34.940
Yeah. This game comes from Atari.

00:17:35.260 --> 00:17:38.460
So they always, you know, they were like the Kings of Arcade back then.

00:17:38.540 --> 00:17:39.600
And it, it's fine, right?

00:17:39.700 --> 00:17:43.540
Like that's a, that's great implementation of a way to get people to spend more money on

00:17:43.540 --> 00:17:48.880
your arcade games. And when they only cost a quarter, it makes sense, but you got to change that stuff when

00:17:48.880 --> 00:17:50.480
you port the stuff over to the NES.

00:17:50.960 --> 00:17:54.340
Uh, one more thing about the arcade cabinet though, really cool arcade cabinet.

00:17:54.340 --> 00:17:57.840
If you guys get a chance to look it up, I'll try to include a link in the show notes

00:17:57.840 --> 00:17:59.880
here. It's a normal arcade cabinet.

00:17:59.880 --> 00:18:06.320
And then they just like mounted a big boom box on top of it where the music actually plays

00:18:06.320 --> 00:18:07.420
out of the boom box.

00:18:07.580 --> 00:18:11.480
So that's like a, a very pleasing late eighties, early nineties vibe.

00:18:12.060 --> 00:18:13.260
It's very radical.

00:18:14.060 --> 00:18:15.920
But no cool control scheme either.

00:18:15.920 --> 00:18:22.360
It's not like, um, you know, you don't use, uh, it's just a joystick in the, in the center

00:18:22.360 --> 00:18:26.460
and then the two buttons for jumping and, um, and pushing forward.

00:18:26.660 --> 00:18:30.620
So you don't step on like a mechanical, uh, skateboard analog.

00:18:31.080 --> 00:18:33.980
No. And there had to be an arcade game that eventually did that, right?

00:18:34.060 --> 00:18:37.920
Like you step on a skateboard and didn't Tony Hawk eventually release something for

00:18:37.920 --> 00:18:39.600
consoles where you step on a skateboard?

00:18:39.740 --> 00:18:40.840
Tony Hawk eight or something.

